# OPTIONAL telemetry-export extras — the OpenTelemetry SDK + OTLP/HTTP exporter (source spec for pip-compile).
#
# This is the INPUT. The hash-pinned, fully-resolved output lives beside it at otel.lock.txt.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE, OPT-IN LOCK. `vigil engage --otlp-endpoint ...` (NET-2b) and the F11 OTLPSink
# (integration/vigil_integration/live/otel_export.py) export spans through
# `opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http`. Neither runtime lock carried it, so on every locked install the
# exporter was absent and telemetry export was non-functional (the CLI and `vigil doctor` say so honestly).
# Telemetry export is OPTIONAL and default-OFF, so its packages stay OUT of the runtime locks (adding a
# network exporter to every deployment would widen the shipped surface for a feature most installs never
# turn on). This lock makes the opt-in path REPRODUCIBLE and hash-enforced instead of `pip install <latest>`:
#
#     VIGIL_WITH_OTEL=1 bash envs/build_envs.sh     # layers it into env-offense, --require-hashes
#     (by hand: after env-offense's locks, `--require-hashes -r infra/supply-chain/otel.lock.txt` with that env's
#      installer — `uv pip install --python .venv-offense/bin/python ...` for a uv-built env, which has no pip)
#
# LAYERING. It is installed into env-offense ONLY (where `vigil` runs), ON TOP of the offense framework lock,
# which is a CONSTRAINT below — so every package this lock shares with it (requests, urllib3, typing-extensions,
# certifi, idna, charset-normalizer) resolves to the IDENTICAL pin and the second --require-hashes install never
# changes a version the first one pinned. integration/tests/test_supply_chain.py asserts that agreement offline
# against env-offense's EFFECTIVE pins (strix.lock overlaid by the framework lock — the build_envs.sh order).
#
# The sovereign lock is ALSO a constraint, so the OpenTelemetry API and protobuf this lock pins are the SAME
# release env-sovereign ships (it pulls opentelemetry-api in via mcp) — one OpenTelemetry version across the
# product. That is safe only because sovereign.in carries the opentelemetry-api>=1.45.1 security floor
# (GHSA-xcq7-p3pf-p88h / CVE-2026-114603); this file restates the floor so it holds even if that one moves.
#
# Regenerate FROM THE REPO ROOT, under Python 3.13 (pip-compile writes `# via` paths relative to its CWD):
#
#     cd <repo root>
#     pip install pip-tools==7.6.1
#     pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-header --strip-extras \
#                 --output-file=infra/supply-chain/otel.lock.txt \
#                 infra/supply-chain/otel.in
#
# The "A14 supply-chain gate" re-runs that command against the committed lock and fails on drift.
# INVARIANT: nothing here may drag in framework.* (CRUCIBLE) or strix.* (FATAL-2) — these are third-party
# telemetry packages only.

-c ../../engine/crucible/framework/v2/requirements.lock.txt
-c sovereign.lock.txt

opentelemetry-sdk
opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http
# Security floor: GHSA-xcq7-p3pf-p88h / CVE-2026-114603 (TraceState.from_header ReDoS) is fixed in 1.45.1.
opentelemetry-api>=1.45.1
